1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,507
A caviar tin is a small thing.

2
00:00:02,557 --> 00:00:04,834
It fits in the palm of your hand.

3
00:00:04,884 --> 00:00:08,977
But every tin that leaves one small building in Ocean Isle Beach,

4
00:00:09,027 --> 00:00:11,531
North Carolina, carries a long story.

5
00:00:11,581 --> 00:00:14,086
It starts in the cold water of Maine.

6
00:00:14,136 --> 00:00:16,924
It runs through the fish markets of Japan.

7
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:20,897
And it ends on plates in some of the best kitchens in America.

8
00:00:22,047 --> 00:00:26,786
This is the story of Great Atlantic Trading, and of its brand, Caviar Star.

9
00:00:26,836 --> 00:00:30,941
It is the story of a husband and wife who started out as divers.

10
00:00:30,991 --> 00:00:39,596
And of a family business that has spent more than three decades learning to do one thing very well: bring the world's finest roe to the table,

11
00:00:39,646 --> 00:00:42,308
and be honest about where it came from.

12
00:00:47,458 --> 00:00:48,898
Portland, Maine.

13
00:00:48,948 --> 00:00:51,071
The late nineteen eighties.

14
00:00:51,121 --> 00:00:54,858
Off this coast, on rocky bottoms just below the kelp,

15
00:00:54,908 --> 00:00:58,396
lives a small spiny animal: the green sea urchin.

16
00:00:58,446 --> 00:01:01,624
For years, almost nobody in Maine wanted it.

17
00:01:01,674 --> 00:01:07,087
Before 1985, the whole state harvested only about forty five metric tons a year,

18
00:01:07,137 --> 00:01:10,564
mostly for small markets in Boston and New York.

19
00:01:11,714 --> 00:01:13,823
Then the world came looking.

20
00:01:13,873 --> 00:01:19,040
In Japan, there was a great appetite for uni, the golden roe inside the urchin.

21
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:23,357
It sold fresh, at premium prices, at the fish auctions of Tokyo.

22
00:01:23,407 --> 00:01:26,655
Japan's own waters could not supply the demand.

23
00:01:26,705 --> 00:01:30,793
Buyers had already turned to California and British Columbia.

24
00:01:30,843 --> 00:01:34,451
As those beds were fished down, they turned to Maine.

25
00:01:35,601 --> 00:01:37,235
The boom was fast.

26
00:01:37,285 --> 00:01:41,121
In 1987, Maine landed 1.4 million pounds of urchins.

27
00:01:41,171 --> 00:01:45,525
In 1990, the catch was worth almost fifteen million dollars.

28
00:01:45,575 --> 00:01:49,606
By 1992, the landings passed twenty six million pounds.

29
00:01:49,656 --> 00:01:54,139
That year, the state began requiring a special urchin license,

30
00:01:54,189 --> 00:01:58,349
and issued eight hundred and seven hand-fishing licenses.

31
00:01:58,399 --> 00:02:02,559
A year later there were fourteen hundred and thirty nine.

32
00:02:02,609 --> 00:02:05,733
Divers and small boats crowded the coast.

33
00:02:05,783 --> 00:02:08,842
Among them were Dana and Denise Leavitt.

34
00:02:13,906 --> 00:02:18,352
Dana and Denise were husband and wife, and they were commercial divers.

35
00:02:18,402 --> 00:02:20,970
They dove for sea urchin and scallops.

36
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:25,068
And they sold the catch themselves, right on the Portland docks.

37
00:02:25,118 --> 00:02:29,848
Years later, the family described that start in a few words: early mornings,

38
00:02:29,898 --> 00:02:33,548
cold waters, and an uncompromising respect for the ocean.

39
00:02:33,598 --> 00:02:36,735
They called the business Great Atlantic Seafood.

40
00:02:37,885 --> 00:02:40,434
Urchin diving is cold, careful work.

41
00:02:40,484 --> 00:02:44,451
The best urchins live in shallow water, just under the kelp.

42
00:02:44,501 --> 00:02:46,460
Their spines are delicate.

43
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:50,063
A broken spine lets salt water in and spoils the roe.

44
00:02:50,113 --> 00:02:53,903
And the best season in Maine runs from November to March.

45
00:02:53,953 --> 00:02:55,203
Winter diving.

46
00:02:56,353 --> 00:03:01,746
At first, most Maine urchins were flown to Japan alive, whole, in their shells.

47
00:03:01,796 --> 00:03:07,752
Around 1992, plants in Portland began cracking them open and flying out the roe instead.

48
00:03:07,802 --> 00:03:12,007
Either way, for the Japanese market, quality was everything.

49
00:03:12,057 --> 00:03:13,133
Freshness.

50
00:03:13,183 --> 00:03:14,009
Color.

51
00:03:14,059 --> 00:03:14,947
Weight.

52
00:03:14,997 --> 00:03:17,012
How the roe was prepared.

53
00:03:17,062 --> 00:03:19,827
Pale yellow earned the highest grade.

54
00:03:19,877 --> 00:03:30,526
Maine's own fishery scientists wrote that the market did not get off the ground until Japanese representatives came to Maine to explain what their market required.

55
00:03:31,676 --> 00:03:33,856
The Leavitts learned from them.

56
00:03:33,906 --> 00:03:40,259
A distributor's profile of the company puts it simply: they were trained by their Japanese counterparts,

57
00:03:40,309 --> 00:03:43,117
with strict quality control at every step.

58
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:46,776
Their small local operation grew into a global business.

59
00:03:46,826 --> 00:03:49,349
Most of what they sold went to Japan.

60
00:03:50,499 --> 00:03:55,101
Only a few photographs survive from those years, and most carry no date.

61
00:03:55,151 --> 00:03:57,019
A dinner, glasses raised.

62
00:03:57,069 --> 00:04:01,904
A busy street in Japan, with a camera date stamp from the nineteen nineties.

63
00:04:01,954 --> 00:04:05,857
What they show is a working relationship across the Pacific.

64
00:04:05,907 --> 00:04:07,718
Japan taught a standard.

65
00:04:07,768 --> 00:04:13,940
And that care for quality, the company says, carried into everything it would later do with caviar.

66
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:21,093
Urchin was the beginning.

67
00:04:21,143 --> 00:04:23,209
Caviar became the passion.

68
00:04:23,259 --> 00:04:28,186
Alongside the uni, the Leavitts began to work with American wild caviar,

69
00:04:28,236 --> 00:04:31,546
and in time they sent caviar to Japan as well.

70
00:04:31,596 --> 00:04:35,030
In 1991, Great Atlantic Trading was established.

71
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:38,079
That year is still on the company's logo.

72
00:04:39,229 --> 00:04:44,295
American wild caviar comes from the great rivers in the middle of the country.

73
00:04:44,345 --> 00:04:48,637
Hackleback, a small sturgeon of the Missouri and the Mississippi.

74
00:04:48,687 --> 00:04:50,659
Paddlefish, the spoonbill.

75
00:04:50,709 --> 00:04:53,514
Bowfin, known in Louisiana as choupique.

76
00:04:53,564 --> 00:04:58,689
These are the roes Great Atlantic still lists among its American exports today.

77
00:04:58,739 --> 00:05:03,864
The company says it has delivered thousands of shipments to customers overseas.

78
00:05:05,014 --> 00:05:07,479
The urchin boom did not last forever.

79
00:05:07,529 --> 00:05:14,522
By the mid nineteen nineties, people in the industry were already worried that the urchin beds were being fished down.

80
00:05:14,572 --> 00:05:18,491
For the Leavitts, caviar and fine roe were taking center stage.

81
00:05:19,641 --> 00:05:22,539
At some point, the family moved south.

82
00:05:22,589 --> 00:05:25,808
The exact year is not in the public record.

83
00:05:25,858 --> 00:05:30,615
But the business settled where it still is today: 563 Seaside Road,

84
00:05:30,665 --> 00:05:35,038
in Ocean Isle Beach, on the southern coast of North Carolina.

85
00:05:40,188 --> 00:05:46,532
To understand what Great Atlantic became, you have to understand what happened to caviar itself.

86
00:05:46,582 --> 00:05:52,495
For generations, the finest caviar came from wild sturgeon in the Caspian and Black Seas.

87
00:05:52,545 --> 00:05:53,418
Beluga.

88
00:05:53,468 --> 00:05:54,340
Osetra.

89
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,323
Sevruga.

90
00:05:55,373 --> 00:05:57,414
Sturgeon are ancient fish.

91
00:05:57,464 --> 00:06:02,209
They can live up to a hundred years, and they do not spawn every year.

92
00:06:02,259 --> 00:06:04,853
They were fished close to the edge.

93
00:06:06,003 --> 00:06:08,715
The rules tightened, step by step.

94
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:15,358
On April first, 1998, every species of sturgeon and paddlefish in the world came under CITES,

95
00:06:15,408 --> 00:06:18,647
the treaty on trade in endangered species.

96
00:06:18,697 --> 00:06:23,316
From then on, caviar could not cross a border without a permit.

97
00:06:23,366 --> 00:06:28,315
In 2004, the United States listed the beluga sturgeon as threatened.

98
00:06:28,365 --> 00:06:34,826
And on September thirtieth, 2005, it stopped imports of beluga caviar from the Caspian Sea.

99
00:06:35,976 --> 00:06:44,770
In 2010, the International Union for Conservation of Nature found that eighty five percent of sturgeon species were at risk of extinction.

100
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:49,374
That made them the most threatened group of animals on its Red List.

101
00:06:50,524 --> 00:06:54,278
So the world's caviar had to come from somewhere else.

102
00:06:54,328 --> 00:06:55,934
It came from farms.

103
00:06:55,984 --> 00:06:59,922
Today, China produces more caviar than any other country.

104
00:06:59,972 --> 00:07:07,407
Farms in Italy, France, Poland, Israel and California raise sturgeon for many years before a single tin is filled.

105
00:07:08,557 --> 00:07:13,207
In this world, an importer has to know exactly what is in every tin.

106
00:07:13,257 --> 00:07:14,444
The species.

107
00:07:14,494 --> 00:07:15,619
The origin.

108
00:07:15,669 --> 00:07:16,794
The permit.

109
00:07:16,844 --> 00:07:24,462
Great Atlantic's export page spells it out: every sturgeon and paddlefish lot carries its CITES number on the label,

110
00:07:24,512 --> 00:07:27,368
and a copy of the permit stays on file.

111
00:07:27,418 --> 00:07:34,295
The company says it is inspected every year, and works under the rules of the Fish and Wildlife Service,

112
00:07:34,345 --> 00:07:37,758
NOAA, the Department of Agriculture and the FDA.

113
00:07:38,908 --> 00:07:42,587
Wild American caviar still exists, in small amounts.

114
00:07:42,637 --> 00:07:50,542
Hackleback is, in the company's words, the only wild sturgeon species still commercially harvested in the United States.

115
00:07:50,592 --> 00:07:53,090
It depends on rivers and weather.

116
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:58,496
In the winter of 2022, low water on the Mississippi kept the fish out of reach.

117
00:07:58,546 --> 00:08:04,835
The company's bowfin fishermen in Louisiana caught less than ten percent of their usual quota.

118
00:08:04,885 --> 00:08:10,242
Its partners said they had not seen conditions this poor in almost two decades.

119
00:08:11,392 --> 00:08:13,728
Politics reaches the tin too.

120
00:08:13,778 --> 00:08:21,080
When war came to Ukraine in 2022, customers asked the family whether any of their caviar came from Russia.

121
00:08:21,130 --> 00:08:24,175
The answer they published was plain: no.

122
00:08:24,225 --> 00:08:33,203
In the same note, they said they were donating caviar to chefs' events raising money for Ukrainian relief and World Central Kitchen.

123
00:08:38,353 --> 00:08:42,340
Most of Great Atlantic's business is with other businesses.

124
00:08:42,390 --> 00:08:43,605
Distributors.

125
00:08:43,655 --> 00:08:44,810
Wholesalers.

126
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:46,437
Chefs and caterers.

127
00:08:46,487 --> 00:08:49,992
But it also built a name of its own for the public.

128
00:08:50,042 --> 00:08:51,980
That name is Caviar Star.

129
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:56,541
No one has published the date Caviar Star began.

130
00:08:56,591 --> 00:09:03,894
But by December 2011, a food writer in New York was building a Christmas Eve dinner around five of its caviars,

131
00:09:03,944 --> 00:09:07,973
among them osetra, paddlefish and American white sturgeon.

132
00:09:08,023 --> 00:09:11,557
Today Caviar Star sells online across the country,

133
00:09:11,607 --> 00:09:15,759
packed to order and shipped overnight from Ocean Isle Beach.

134
00:09:16,909 --> 00:09:20,656
The idea behind the brand is written on its own pages.

135
00:09:20,706 --> 00:09:26,596
"Caviar is a luxury, but at Caviar Star, it's a luxury meant to be enjoyed." True luxury,

136
00:09:26,646 --> 00:09:30,331
the family writes, should be inviting, not exclusive.

137
00:09:30,381 --> 00:09:34,618
They buy directly from farms and fisheries, without middlemen,

138
00:09:34,668 --> 00:09:38,108
and import in volume to keep prices within reach.

139
00:09:39,258 --> 00:09:41,702
Inside, the work is done by hand.

140
00:09:41,752 --> 00:09:44,744
Every container that arrives is inspected.

141
00:09:44,794 --> 00:09:46,690
The caviar is re-graded.

142
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:54,233
Then it is hand-packed to order, every day, from large original tins into jars and tins from one ounce up to a kilo.

143
00:09:54,283 --> 00:09:59,951
The company describes more than eight thousand five hundred square feet of production,

144
00:10:00,001 --> 00:10:04,817
office and warehouse space, with three walk-in coolers and two freezers.

145
00:10:05,967 --> 00:10:12,533
In 2021, the magazine The Local Palate named Caviar Star among the South's best caviar.

146
00:10:12,583 --> 00:10:18,661
It reported that the family's small team moves nearly ten tons of caviar a year.

147
00:10:19,811 --> 00:10:29,660
Perishable orders go out by UPS next day air, in insulated boxes the company says keep caviar cold for up to seventy two hours.

148
00:10:29,710 --> 00:10:33,692
The family has also tried to ship more lightly.

149
00:10:33,742 --> 00:10:41,684
In 2018, Caviar Star began using boxes insulated with recycled cotton and denim instead of Styrofoam.

150
00:10:41,734 --> 00:10:46,230
In 2023, it moved to carbon-neutral shipping with UPS.

151
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:54,740
Open the 2026 catalog and the range is wide.

152
00:10:54,790 --> 00:10:57,167
Domestic and imported caviar.

153
00:10:57,217 --> 00:11:00,643
Serving pieces in mother of pearl and silver.

154
00:11:00,693 --> 00:11:02,480
Truffles from Italy.

155
00:11:02,530 --> 00:11:04,972
Oils and vinegars from Europe.

156
00:11:05,022 --> 00:11:06,087
Escargot.

157
00:11:06,137 --> 00:11:07,071
Cheese.

158
00:11:07,121 --> 00:11:12,842
The origin key alone lists fourteen countries, from the United States to France,

159
00:11:12,892 --> 00:11:15,597
Poland, Denmark, China and Turkey.

160
00:11:16,747 --> 00:11:20,451
Some of these products carry their own places with them.

161
00:11:20,501 --> 00:11:24,733
From the Mississippi basin, wild hackleback, small and jet black,

162
00:11:24,783 --> 00:11:27,665
and paddlefish, steel gray to olive green.

163
00:11:27,715 --> 00:11:31,889
Bowfin, from fishermen working between Baton Rouge and Illinois,

164
00:11:31,939 --> 00:11:35,877
a partnership the company says goes back more than a decade.

165
00:11:35,927 --> 00:11:39,572
And from Sacramento, California, farmed white sturgeon,

166
00:11:39,622 --> 00:11:42,974
from the largest freshwater fish in North America.

167
00:11:44,124 --> 00:11:50,116
From abroad, French osetra from the Caviar House and Prunier farm near Bordeaux.

168
00:11:50,166 --> 00:11:56,089
Prunier opened what it calls the world's first dedicated caviar house, in 1921.

169
00:11:56,139 --> 00:12:02,474
Great Atlantic says it is the exclusive importer of that caviar in the United States.

170
00:12:02,524 --> 00:12:07,624
Osetra and Siberian caviar from a farm on the Lyna River in Poland,

171
00:12:07,674 --> 00:12:09,821
in operation since 1967.

172
00:12:09,871 --> 00:12:15,657
And Kaluga hybrid from Kaluga Queen, the largest caviar company in the world.

173
00:12:16,807 --> 00:12:20,074
Then there is one product the family made its own.

174
00:12:20,124 --> 00:12:26,194
Rainbow trout roe, salted, and cold-smoked slowly over chips from Pappy Van Winkle bourbon barrels.

175
00:12:26,244 --> 00:12:29,626
The company says it was the first to smoke fish roe,

176
00:12:29,676 --> 00:12:34,316
back in the two thousands, and that it still does it without liquid smoke.

177
00:12:35,466 --> 00:12:38,212
The pantry has its own long histories.

178
00:12:38,262 --> 00:12:44,167
A balsamic vinegar from Modena that has passed through wooden barrels for a hundred years,

179
00:12:44,217 --> 00:12:47,266
made to a family recipe that dates to 1896.

180
00:12:47,316 --> 00:12:50,244
It comes with a certificate and a number.

181
00:12:50,294 --> 00:12:53,708
And one more, a quiet link to where it all began.

182
00:12:53,758 --> 00:12:58,935
Maine lobster, wild-caught off the same coast where Dana and Denise once dove.

183
00:13:01,237 --> 00:13:02,647
Who eats it all?

184
00:13:02,697 --> 00:13:06,419
The company names its customers plainly: distributors,

185
00:13:06,469 --> 00:13:09,643
wholesalers, chefs, caterers, and home cooks.

186
00:13:09,693 --> 00:13:19,499
In Charleston, South Carolina, chef James London's restaurant Chubby Fish serves what Caviar Star calls a now-famous caviar sandwich: warm buttered bread,

187
00:13:19,549 --> 00:13:23,088
cool crème fraîche, and a generous layer of caviar.

188
00:13:23,138 --> 00:13:27,042
Caviar Star says its Kaluga is the same one served there,

189
00:13:27,092 --> 00:13:30,692
and it has published a version you can make at home.

190
00:13:35,842 --> 00:13:39,798
Through all of it, Great Atlantic has stayed a family business.

191
00:13:39,848 --> 00:13:44,538
Today, the company says, Dana and Denise run it with their son and daughter.

192
00:13:44,588 --> 00:13:50,124
One post on their website puts it in a single line: "family owned and operated by a mother,

193
00:13:50,174 --> 00:13:52,099
father, son, and daughter."

194
00:13:53,249 --> 00:13:56,607
The next generation's names are on the work.

195
00:13:56,657 --> 00:14:02,243
Deanna Leavitt published the 2026 catalog and writes for the Caviar Star blog.

196
00:14:02,293 --> 00:14:05,192
Derek Leavitt has written for it too.

197
00:14:05,242 --> 00:14:08,796
Dana's own name is on posts going back to 2015.

198
00:14:08,846 --> 00:14:16,660
And on one distributor's page, Deanna's line is short and to the point: "Life is too short for mediocre caviar."

199
00:14:17,810 --> 00:14:20,938
The years ahead bring familiar challenges.

200
00:14:20,988 --> 00:14:25,386
Wild American roe depends on rivers that run low in a drought.

201
00:14:25,436 --> 00:14:31,995
In 2026, the family warned customers of a shortage of Alaskan salmon roe after a very low catch.

202
00:14:32,045 --> 00:14:35,872
Rules, tariffs and farm harvests change every season.

203
00:14:35,922 --> 00:14:40,574
The work stays the same: check every tin, know where it came from,

204
00:14:40,624 --> 00:14:43,561
and be open with the customer about it.

205
00:14:44,711 --> 00:14:54,858
In the company's own words: "We have always been passionate about holding up the integrity of each individual species and being transparent about where it's coming from."

206
00:15:00,008 --> 00:15:04,756
It began with two divers in cold Maine water, and a catch sold on the dock.

207
00:15:04,806 --> 00:15:08,803
It grew through Japan, through the end of wild Caspian caviar,

208
00:15:08,853 --> 00:15:12,503
and through the rise of sturgeon farms around the world.

209
00:15:12,553 --> 00:15:16,261
By the family's count, more than thirty five years later,

210
00:15:16,311 --> 00:15:21,869
it is still a family business, still packing tins by hand on the coast of North Carolina.

211
00:15:23,019 --> 00:15:24,857
The tin is still small.

212
00:15:24,907 --> 00:15:26,988
The story inside it is not.

